course: biology
lesson: 220
title: Spiders and other minibeasts under logs and stones
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On a misty autumn morning the garden is full of spider webs shining with dew, each one spun from silk that comes out of little nozzles called spinnerets at the back of the spider.
explain: The spider waits in a corner with one foot on a thread, and when a fly hits the web it feels the shaking, like someone feeling a phone buzz in their pocket.
explain: Lift a log or a stone and a whole hidden town scurries away: grey woodlice, which are cousins of crabs, fast hunting centipedes, slow millipedes that curl into a coil, and shiny beetles.
explain: Most of these minibeasts eat dead leaves and rotting wood, so they are the garden's recycling team, turning old plants back into soil.
explain: A spider has eight legs and is not an insect, a woodlouse has fourteen legs, and centipedes and millipedes have many more, so counting legs is the quickest way to tell them apart.
fact: Spiders make silk in their bodies and spin it out of spinnerets; they make different silks for different jobs, such as sticky threads, strong frame threads and egg sacs.
fact: A garden spider often waits in a corner of its web holding a signal thread, and feels the web shake when an insect is caught.
fact: Many spiders eat their old web before building a new one, to reuse the protein in the silk.
fact: A spider in the bath has usually fallen in and cannot climb out, because the sides are too smooth; it did not come up the plughole.
fact: The idea that people swallow spiders in their sleep is a myth; a sleeping person breathes, snores and has a beating heart, and those vibrations warn spiders away.
fact: In autumn many house spiders run across the floor because the males are wandering in search of females.
fact: Woodlice need damp places and hide under logs and stones; they are crustaceans, related to crabs and shrimps, not insects.
fact: Centipedes are fast hunters with one pair of legs on each body segment and venom claws to catch insects.
fact: Millipedes are slow, eat dead plants, have two pairs of legs on most body segments, and curl into a tight coil to protect themselves.
fact: Beetles are insects with six legs; beetles that live under stones help to break down dead plants and animals.
fact: A log pile in a corner of the garden is a mini habitat that shelters many minibeasts, and frogs and hedgehogs come to hunt there.
fact: A spider has eight legs and two body parts, while an insect has six legs and three body parts; a woodlouse has fourteen legs.
fact: Despite their name, no centipede has exactly one hundred legs, and most millipedes have far fewer than a thousand.
exercise: Where does a spider's silk come out? | From spinnerets at the back of its body | From its mouth, a bit like spit | From the tips of its eight legs | From tiny holes just below its eyes
exercise: Why does a spider make different kinds of silk? | Each kind does a different job, like sticky threads or egg sacs | It only makes one kind, but in different colours | To match the colour of the flowers | To confuse people who look closely at the web
exercise: How does a spider in the corner of its web know a fly is caught? | It feels the web shake | It hears the fly shouting | It sees it with eyes on its back | It smells it from far away
exercise: Why do many spiders eat their own old web? | To reuse the protein in the silk | Because they cannot catch any flies | To clean their fangs after a meal | Because the old silk tastes sweet
exercise: How did the spider most likely get into the bath? | It fell in and cannot climb the smooth sides | It climbed up through the plughole from the drains | It was born in the bath water | It came in through the taps
exercise: Is it true that people swallow lots of spiders in their sleep? | No, it is a myth; breathing and heartbeats warn spiders away | Yes, about eight spiders a year, as scientists have counted | Yes, but only in autumn | Yes, spiders like warm mouths
exercise: Why do you see big house spiders running across the carpet in autumn? | Males are wandering to find females | They are running away from winter snow | They are looking for a bath to drink from | They are chasing people's feet
exercise: Why do woodlice hide under logs and stones? | They need damp places or they dry out | They are hiding from the bright moon at night | They can lay their eggs only on cold stone | They are too cold to stay out in the sun
exercise: What are woodlice related to? | Crabs and shrimps | Ants and honey bees | Spiders and scorpions | Earthworms and slugs
exercise: How does a centipede catch its food? | It hunts fast and grabs with venom claws | It spins a sticky web between the stones | It waits for leaves to fall | It sucks sap from the roots of plants
exercise: What does a millipede do when it is in danger? | It curls up into a tight coil | It runs away very fast | It bites with venom claws | It jumps high into the air
exercise: How can you tell a millipede from a centipede? | Millipedes have two pairs of legs on most segments | Millipedes have exactly a thousand legs | Centipedes have no legs at all | Centipedes are always much slower than millipedes
exercise: What do beetles under stones do for the garden? | Help break down dead plants and animals | Make the stones grow bigger | Eat all the worms in the soil until none are left | Keep the frogs away
exercise: Why is a log pile good for a garden? | It shelters many minibeasts and the animals that hunt them | It keeps all the insects out of the garden | It makes the grass grow faster | It stops the rain from falling in that corner of the garden
exercise: How many legs does a spider have? | Eight | Twelve | Twenty | Fourteen
exercise: Is a spider an insect? | No, insects have six legs and spiders have eight | Yes, all small crawling animals are insects | Yes, because it has wings when it is young | No, spiders are a kind of crab
test: «there's like 4 spiders in the shed, how many legs is that, gross» | 32 | 24 | 16 | 40
test: «my cousin says u swallow 8 spiders a year when ur asleep» What is right? | It is a myth; spiders avoid sleeping, breathing people | True, it is a proven fact | True, but only in summer | True, but you breathe them out again before you wake up
test: In a cartoon an itsy-bitsy spider climbs up the drain pipe. What is true about a spider found in the bath? | It probably fell in from above and cannot climb out | It certainly came up the drain from the sewers | It was waiting under the water all night | It came out of the bath tap when it was turned on
test: «found a huge spider running round the living room last night, it's attacking us!» What is it most likely doing? | A male searching for a female in autumn | Hunting people to bite | Running away from the cat | Looking for its lost web in the carpet by the sofa
test: Count them: a woodlouse has 14 legs. How many legs do 3 woodlice have? | 42 | 17 | 24 | 36
test: What would most likely happen to woodlice if you moved their log into a hot sunny spot? | They would dry out unless they found a damp place | They would love the sun and grow bigger | They would turn into beetles | They would build a new log
test: In a famous story a centipede has a hundred legs and wears lots of boots. What is true? | No centipede has exactly one hundred legs | Every centipede has exactly one hundred legs | Centipedes have no legs, only bristles | Centipedes have exactly eight legs
test: «ugh a centipede and a millipede, which one's the hunter» | The centipede, fast with venom claws | The millipede, fast with a sticky web | Both of them hunt only small frogs at night | Neither, both of them eat only dead leaves
test: What would happen to a garden spider's web if no flies came for a whole week? | The spider would go hungry and might eat and rebuild the web | The web would catch flies by itself | The spider would turn into a fly | The web would keep on growing bigger and stronger all on its own
test: «lift the stone, quick, what's under there» Which animals are you most likely to find? | Woodlice, beetles and centipedes | Butterflies and bees | Spiders' eggs only | Snakes and lizards only, curled up asleep
test: Count them: a spider and a beetle sit on a log. How many legs altogether? | 14 | 12 | 16 | 10
exam: Identify the means by which a spider situated at the margin of its web detects the capture of prey. | It perceives the vibration transmitted along the threads | It hears the vocalisations of the captured insect | It observes the web with eyes situated upon its dorsal surface | It detects the odour of the prey at a considerable distance
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the claim that persons ingest spiders during sleep? | It is a myth unsupported by evidence, as spiders avoid sleeping persons | It is an established fact, the figure being eight spiders annually | It occurs exclusively during the autumn months | It occurs only in dwellings situated near woodland
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of the woodlouse. | It is a crustacean and is therefore related to crabs and shrimps | It is an insect and is therefore related to ants and bees | It is an arachnid and is therefore related to spiders and scorpions | It is a mollusc and is therefore related to slugs
exam: Ascertain the principal difference in feeding between the centipede and the millipede. | The centipede hunts living prey, whereas the millipede consumes dead plant matter | The millipede hunts living prey, whereas the centipede consumes dead plant matter | Both are exclusively consumers of living plants | Both obtain nourishment by the spinning of webs
